"CSS Cascading and Inheritance Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-cascade-5/)\nciteturn1288view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-cascade-5/\",\"lineno\":754}); Total lines: 1419\nL725: Note: Since layer ordering is defined by first occurrence of the layer name (see cite57†§ 6.4.3 Layer Ordering ), this rule allows a page to declare the order of its layers up front, so that their order is apparent without having to read the entire style sheet. It also allows inline layers to be interleaved with imported layers, which is not possible with the cite59†block syntax .\nL726: The statement syntax allows establishing a layer order in advance, regardless of the order in which style rules are added to each layer. It can be helpful to establish that layer order in advance, before any cite121†@import rules. In this example, the imported `theme.css` style rules will override any rules added in the later default block since the order of layers has already been established:\nL727: \nL728:     @layer default, theme, components;\nL729:     @import url(theme.css) layer(theme);\nL730: \nL731:     @layer default {\nL732:       audio[controls] {\nL733:         display: block;\nL734:       }\nL735:     }\nL736: It’s also possible to have cite121†@import rules help establish the order, by placing them between cite135†@layer rules. This example will have the same result:\nL737: \nL738:     @layer default;\nL739:     @import url(theme.css) layer(theme);\nL740:     @layer components;\nL741: \nL742:     @layer default {\nL743:       audio[controls] {\nL744:         display: block;\nL745:       }\nL746:     }\nL747: However, cite121†@import and cite122†@namespace rules must be consecutive, without any intervening rules. The following is invalid:\nL748: \nL749:     @import url(default.css) layer(default);\nL750:     @layer theme;\nL751:     @import url(components.css) layer(components);\nL752: \nL753:     @layer theme {\nL754:       audio[controls] {\nL755:         display: block;\nL756:       }\nL757:     }\nL758: ### 6.5. Precedence of Non-CSS Presentational Hints\nL759: The UA may choose to honor presentational hints in a source document’s markup, for example the `bgcolor` attribute or `cite294†s†html.spec.whatwg.org ` element in cite274†[HTML] . All document language-based styling must be translated to corresponding CSS rules and enter the cascade as rules in either the cite259†UA-origin or a special-purpose author presentational hint origin between the regular cite260†user origin and the cite261†author origin .\nL760: For the purpose of cite111†cascading this cite295†author presentational hint origin is treated as an independent cite151†origin ; however for the purpose of the cite296†revert keyword (but not for the cite27†revert-layer keyword) it is considered part of the author origin.\nL761: A document language may define whether such a presentational hint enters the cite111†cascade as cite259†UA-origin or cite261†author-origin ; if so, the UA must behave accordingly. For example, cite297†[SVG11] maps its presentation attributes into the author origin.\nL762: Note: Presentational hints entering the cite111†cascade as cite259†UA-origin rules can be overridden by cite261†author-origin or cite260†user-origin styles. Presentational hints entering the cascade as cite295†author presentational hint origin rules can be overridden by author-origin styles, but not by non-cite257†important user-origin styles. Host languages should choose the appropriate origin for presentational hints with these considerations in mind.\nL763: ## 7. Defaulting\nL764: \nL765: When the cite111†cascade does not result in a value, the cite115†specified value must be found some other way. cite298†Inherited properties draw their defaults from their parent element through cite113†inheritance ; all other properties take their cite114†initial value . Authors can explicitly request inheritance or initialization via the cite299†inherit and cite300†initial keywords.\nL766: ### 7.1. Initial Values\nL767: \nL768: Each property has an initial value, defined in the property’s definition table. If the property is not an cite298†inherited property , and the cite111†cascade does not result in a value, then the cite115†specified value of the property is its cite114†initial value .\nL769: ### 7.2. Inheritance\nL770: \nL771: Inheritance propagates property values from parent elements to their children. The inherited value of a property on an element is the cite203†computed value of the property on the element’s parent element. For the root element, which has no parent element, the cite301†inherited value is the cite114†initial value of the property.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Values and Units Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/)\nciteturn1288view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/\",\"lineno\":2080}); Total lines: 2842\nL2050: CSSOM\nL2051: \nL2052:   * cite1199†variable-reference-cssom.html†wpt.fyi cite1200†(live test)†wpt.live cite1201†(source)†github.com L2053: \nL2054: * * *\nL2055: \nL2056: Note that cite177†substitution takes place at the level of CSS tokens cite1202†[css-syntax-3] , not at a textual level; you can’t build up a single token where part of it is provided by a variable:\nL2057: \nL2058:     .foo {\nL2059:       --gap: 20;\nL2060:       margin-top: var(--gap)px;\nL2061:     }\nL2062: This is not equivalent to setting cite782†margin-top: 20px; (a length). Instead, it’s equivalent to margin-top: 20 px; (a number followed by an ident), which is simply an invalid value for the margin-top property. Note, though, that cite426†calc() can be used to validly achieve the same thing, like so:\nL2063: \nL2064:     .foo {\nL2065:       --gap: 20;\nL2066:       margin-top: calc(var(--gap) * 1px);\nL2067:     }\nL2068: This also implies that the post-substitution value might not be directly serializable as-is. Here’s a similar example to the preceding:\nL2069: \nL2070:     .foo {\nL2071:       --gap: 20;\nL2072:       --not-px-length: var(--gap)px;\nL2073:     }\nL2074: The serialization of the computed (post-substitution) value of --not-px-length is not 20px, because that would parse back as the single combined dimension; instead, it will serialize with a comment between the two tokens, like px, to enforce that they are separate tokens even when re-parsing.\nL2075: \nL2076: Tests\nL2077:   * cite1203†variable-declaration-14.html†wpt.fyi cite1204†(live test)†wpt.live cite1205†(source)†github.com L2078:   * cite1206†variable-declaration-53.html†wpt.fyi cite1207†(live test)†wpt.live cite1208†(source)†github.com L2079:   * cite1209†variable-declaration-54.html†wpt.fyi cite1210†(live test)†wpt.live cite1211†(source)†github.com L2080:   * cite1212†variable-declaration-55.html†wpt.fyi cite1213†(live test)†wpt.live cite1214†(source)†github.com L2081:   * cite1215†variable-reference-15.html†wpt.fyi cite1216†(live test)†wpt.live cite1217†(source)†github.com L2082:   * cite1218†variable-reference-without-whitespace.html†wpt.fyi cite1219†(live test)†wpt.live cite1220†(source)†github.com L2083: ### Invalid Substitution\nL2084: \nL2085: When cite537†property replacement results in a property’s value containing the cite175†guaranteed-invalid value , this makes the declaration invalid at computed-value time. When this happens, the computed value is one of the following depending on the property’s type:\nL2086: \nL2087: The property is a non-registered cite548†custom property L2088: The property is a cite145†registered custom property†drafts.css-houdini.org with cite169†universal syntax†drafts.css-houdini.org L2089: The computed value is the cite175†guaranteed-invalid value .\nL2090: \nL2091: Otherwise\nL2092: \nL2093: \nL2094: Either the property’s inherited value or its initial value depending on whether the property is inherited or not, respectively, as if the property’s value had been specified as the cite1090†unset keyword.\nL2095: \nL2096: Tests\nL2097: \nL2098:   * cite1221†variables-substitute-guaranteed-invalid.html†wpt.fyi cite1222†(live test)†wpt.live cite1223†(source)†github.com L2099: For example, in the following code:\nL2100: \nL2101:     :root { --not-a-color: 20px; }\nL2102:     p { background-color: red; }\nL2103:     p { background-color: var(--not-a-color); }\nL2104: \nL2105: \nL2106: the <p> elements will have transparent backgrounds (the initial value for cite1121†background-color ), rather than red backgrounds. The same would happen if the cite548†custom property itself was unset, or contained an invalid cite133†var() function.\nL2107: Note the difference between this and what happens if the author had just written cite1121†background-color: 20px directly in their stylesheet - that would be a normal syntax error, which would cause the rule to be discarded, so the background-color: red rule would be used instead.\nL2108: Note: The cite492†invalid at computed-value time concept exists because cite132†arbitrary substitution functions can’t \"fail early\" like other syntax errors can, so by the time the user agent realizes a property value is invalid, it’s already thrown away the other cascaded values.\nL2109: ### Substitution in Shorthand Properties\nL2110: \nL2111: cite132†Arbitrary substitution functions produce some complications when parsing cite547†shorthand properties into their component longhands, and when serializing shorthand properties from their component longhands.\nL2112: If a cite547†shorthand property contains an cite132†arbitrary substitution function in its value, the cite1224†longhand properties it’s associated with must instead be filled in with a special, unobservable-to-authors pending-substitution value that indicates the shorthand contains an arbitrary substitution function, and thus the longhand’s value can’t be determined until after cite177†substituted .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Properties and Values API Level 1 (https://drafts.css-houdini.org/css-properties-values-api-1/)\nciteturn1288view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.css-houdini.org/css-properties-values-api-1/\",\"lineno\":140}); Total lines: 1339\nL135: Further, UA-defined properties have their syntax determined by the version of the UA the user is viewing the page with; this is out of the page author’s control, which is the entire reason for CSS’s error-recovery behavior and the practice of writing multiple declarations for varying levels of support. A custom property, on the other hand, has its syntax controlled by the page author, according to whatever stylesheet or script they’ve included in the page; there’s no unpredictability to be managed.\nL136: Throwing away syntax-violating custom properties would thus only be, at best, a convenience for the page author, not a necessity like for UA-defined properties.\nL137: ### 2.3. cite90†Specified Value†drafts.csswg.org -Time Behavior\nL138: \nL139: Just like unregistered cite77†custom properties†drafts.csswg.org , all cite81†registered custom properties , regardless of registered syntax, accept the cite91†CSS-wide keywords†drafts.csswg.org , such as cite92†inherit†drafts.csswg.org or cite93†revert†drafts.csswg.org . Their behavior is defined in cite94†CSS Cascading 4 § 7.3 Explicit Defaulting†drafts.csswg.org .\nL140: ### 2.4. cite95†Computed Value†drafts.csswg.org -Time Behavior\nL141: \nL142: The cite95†computed value†drafts.csswg.org of a cite81†registered custom property is determined by the syntax of its cite96†registration .\nL143: \nL144: If the cite96†registration’s syntax is the cite97†universal syntax definition , the cite95†computed value†drafts.csswg.org is the same as for unregistered cite77†custom properties†drafts.csswg.org (either the specified value with variables substituted, or the cite87†guaranteed-invalid value†drafts.csswg.org ).\nL145: Otherwise, attempt to cite86†parse†drafts.csswg.org the property’s value according to its registered syntax. If this fails, the declaration is cite98†invalid at computed-value time†drafts.csswg.org and the cite95†computed value†drafts.csswg.org is determined accordingly. If it succeeds, the cite95†computed value†drafts.csswg.org depends on the specifics of the syntax:\nL146: \nL147: For `\"<length>\"`, `\"<length-percentage>\"`, `\"<angle>\"`, `\"<time>\"`, `\"<resolution>\"`, `\"<integer>\"`, `\"<number>\"`, and `\"<percentage>\"` values:\nL148:   * If the specified value is a cite99†dimension†drafts.csswg.org literal (such as 50em or .2s), the computed value is the same value, but with the unit converted to the corresponding cite100†canonical unit†drafts.csswg.org for the type of value.\nL149: \nL150:   * If the specified value is any other numeric literal (such as 5 or 20%), the computed value is as specified. (In particular, percentages are never resolved against anything.)\nL151:   * If the specified value is a function that evaluates to one of those types (such as a cite101†math function†drafts.csswg.org ), the computed value is defined by that function.\nL152: \nL153: For `\"<string>\"` values, the computed value is as specified.\nL154: \nL155: For `\"<color>\"` values, the value is computed by resolving color values, per cite102†CSS Color 4 §  15. Resolving <color> Values†drafts.csswg.org .\nL156: \nL157: For `\"<custom-ident>\"`, ident, or `\"*\"` values, the computed value is as specified.\nL158: For `\"<url>\"` values, the computed value is one of the following:\nL159: \nL160:   * if the URL is a relative URL, the computed value is the resolved absolute URL as described in cite103†[css3-values] .\nL161: \nL162:   * otherwise, the computed value is as specified.\nL163: \nL164: URL behavior examples\nL165: \nL166: Because URLs resolve against the base URL of the stylesheet they appear in, we can end up with multiple relative URLs that resolve against different base URLs, even though they appear in the same property.\nL167: For example, suppose --url-foo and --url-bar are registered custom properties with <url> syntax, and that we have a stylesheet at `/style/foo/foo.css`:\nL168: \nL169:     div {\nL170:       --url-foo: url(\"foo.png\");\nL171:     }\nL172: \nL173: \nL174: and another stylesheet at `/style/bar/bar.css`\nL175: \nL176:     div {\nL177:       --url-bar: url(\"bar.png\");\nL178:     }\nL179: and finally a document at `/index.html`:\nL180: \nL181:     <link href=\"/style/foo/foo.css\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\">\nL182:     <link href=\"/style/bar/bar.css\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\">\nL183:     <div style=\"background-image: var(--url-foo), var(--url-bar);\">\nL184:     </div>\nL185: \nL186: \nL187: Here, the var(--url-foo) reference would produce a URL that resolves against `/style/foo`, and the var(--url-bar) reference would produce a URL that resolves against `/style/bar`.\n"
